# @PIPELINE_NAME@ (@VARIANT_NAME@) app

This app was exported from the Visual Pipeline and Platform Evaluation Tool (ViPPET)
@VIPPET_VERSION@ on @EXPORTED_AT@. It runs the **@PIPELINE_NAME@** pipeline, variant
**@VARIANT_NAME@** (@DEVICES@), on DL Streamer Pipeline Server 2.0, with the model and
input video bundled in `assets/`.

## Requirements

- Linux with Docker and Docker Compose v2, `jq` and `curl`.
@DEVICE_REQUIREMENTS@
- Internet access the first time, to pull `@DLSPS2_IMAGE@` and
  `@MEDIAMTX_IMAGE@`.

## Quick start

```bash
./start_app.sh                    # start the stack
ID=$(./run.sh rtsp)               # start a pipeline instance, print its id
./status.sh "$ID"                 # state and FPS of the instance
./stop.sh "$ID"                   # stop it (./stop.sh with no id stops all)
./stop_app.sh                     # stop all instances and remove the containers
```

`./start_app.sh rtsp` starts the stack and an instance in one step.

## Outputs

`./run.sh [fakesink|file|rtsp] [name]` starts one instance. Its outputs are named after
the instance name, which defaults to `<output>-<date>-<time>`:

| Output | Input video | Writes |
|--------|-------------|--------|
| `fakesink` (default) | loops until stopped | events only |
| `file` | played once | `output/<name>.mp4` |
| `rtsp` | loops until stopped | stream at `rtsp://localhost:18554/<name>` |

Every instance writes its detection events to `output/<name>.jsonl`: one JSON object per
video frame, with the frame resolution, a `timestamp` in nanoseconds of pipeline time
(starting at 0, not wall-clock time) and the detected `objects`. Follow it with
`tail -F output/<name>.jsonl | jq -c .`. A blank line marks the end of a run.

A looping instance writes events until it is stopped, about 0.5 KB per frame (roughly
50 MB per hour at 30 FPS). Stop instances you no longer need. `./run.sh` deletes the
events file of the same name before it starts a new instance.

## Configuration

Set these variables when you run the scripts, e.g. `REST_PORT=28080 ./start_app.sh`:

| Variable | Default | Purpose |
|----------|---------|---------|
| `REST_PORT` | `18080` | DL Streamer Pipeline Server REST API, on `127.0.0.1` only |
| `RTSP_PORT` | `18554` | RTSP output |
| `DLSPS2_IMAGE` | `@DLSPS2_IMAGE@` | Pipeline server image |

The defaults differ from ViPPET's ports, so the app can run on the same host.
`./start_app.sh` saves the ports, your user and the GPU render group to `.env`; the other
scripts and plain `docker compose` commands in this directory read it.

## Files

| Path | Content |
|------|---------|
| `compose.yml` | The stack: DL Streamer Pipeline Server 2.0 and MediaMTX |
| `pipelines.json` | Launch string per output; `{name}` is replaced by the instance name |
| `.env` | Written by `./start_app.sh`: ports, user and render group for `compose.yml` |
| `manifest.json` | Source pipeline and variant, ViPPET version, checksums of the assets |
| `assets/` | Model and video files, mounted read-only at `/models` and `/videos` |
| `output/` | Videos and events written by the instances |

